---
// Body for /services/rescues/ — rescues, rebuilds, migrations.
---

<section class="section section--rule">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Who this is for</span>
      <h2>Three situations that bring people here.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid--3">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>It broke</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          An update took the site down, the contact form quietly died, or the
          whole thing takes eleven seconds to load and nobody knows why.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>They vanished</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          The person who built it stopped answering. Now nobody can log in,
          nothing can be changed, and the invoice keeps auto-billing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>It's held hostage</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          The domain, the hosting, or the whole site sits in someone else's
          account, and every small edit costs money and a week of waiting.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Process</span>
      <h2>Honest diagnosis first. Then the fix.</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <h3>The video audit</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          I record a screen-share walking through your site: what's working,
          what's costing you calls, what's actually broken versus just ugly.
          You watch it on your own time.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Repair or rebuild — the straight answer</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          If a repair solves it, that's the recommendation, even though a
          rebuild pays me more. If a rebuild is the honest answer, you'll get
          a fixed quote and a delivery date in writing.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Nothing gets lost</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          Your content comes out of the old site before anything changes.
          Every old address gets a redirect map, so links that point at your
          site today still land somewhere that works.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Relaunch, in your name</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          The rebuilt site goes live in accounts you own — domain, hosting,
          everything. If those need reclaiming from a vanished developer,
          that's part of the job, handled politely and persistently.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--rule">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Questions</span>
      <h2>Asked often, answered straight.</h2>
    </div>
    <details class="faq">
      <summary>Can you just fix it without a full rebuild?</summary>
      <div class="faq-body">
        <p>
          If the audit says a repair does the job, yes — and that's what I'll
          recommend. Some platforms are slow to work on, so repairs there get
          quoted honestly; if someone else would serve you better for a
          platform-specific fix, I'll say so.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq">
      <summary>We're on WordPress and tired of it.</summary>
      <div class="faq-body">
        <p>
          Moving off WordPress onto a fast static site is bread-and-butter
          work: content comes out, plugins stop mattering, and the monthly
          patch-and-pray routine ends. If you want to stay on WordPress,
          I'll tell you plainly whether I'm the right person for that work.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq">
      <summary>Will our old links and Google listings break?</summary>
      <div class="faq-body">
        <p>
          Every rebuild ships with a redirect map: old addresses forward to
          the right new pages, so bookmarks, citations, and listings keep
          working. That's a standard deliverable, not an extra.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq">
      <summary>Our old developer has our domain. Is it recoverable?</summary>
      <div class="faq-body">
        <p>
          Usually, yes — registrars and hosts have processes for exactly
          this. I'll walk you through the recovery steps and handle the
          technical side. What I can't do is promise a timeline that depends
          on someone else answering email.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
